Classification of Medicines
In the Lao PDR, medicines are classified into the following types:
1. Medicines distributed, sold and used under medical’ s prescription;
2. Medicines distributed, sold and used under a pharmacist’s control;
3. Medicines generally sold to public without any medical’s prescription;
4. Medicines for household use;
5. Toxic, narcotic and dangerous medicines as determined and listed by the
Ministry of Health.

Medicines in Possession
Medicines are allowed to be in possession in the following cases:
1. Medicines used for medical treatment by physicians, dentists or
obstetricians who are permitted to use such medicines for medical
treatment;
2. Medicines permitted for any individual use by patients;
3. Medicines for individual use when travelling;
4. Non-narcotic traditional medicines;
5. Medicines for household use.

Conditions for Business on Production, Export, Import and Wholesale of
Medicines and Medical Products
In addition to the conditions stipulated in the Law on Enterprises, any individuals
or organizations wishing to run the business on production, export, import and wholesale
of medicines and medical products shall be subject to the following conditions:
1. Have a pharmacist with at least five years of experiences;
2. Have never been sentenced for the offenses relating to medicines or
narcotic drugs or medical products;
3. Have an ethic on medicines and business;
4. Have personnel with knowledge and capacity;
5. Have a good health, no mental defects or communicable diseases;
6. Have measures on safety and environment protection;
7. Have an appropriate premises, necessary facilities, standardized
warehouse and transportation vehicles.

For manufacturer, production and laboratory facilities with complete equipments
shall be additionally required.

(New). Prohibitions for Pharmacists and other Health Technicians
The pharmacists and health technicians are prohibited to act as follow:
1. Give pharmaceutical license to others for use;
2. Produce, sell or provide substandard and unsafe medicines and
medical products to others or without physician’s prescription;
3. Misuse his/her own power and position and improperly, unlawfully
induce others to do so in relation to medicines and medical products,
for private gain;
4. Disclose secret information and formulae relating to medicines and
medical products without permission;
5. Assign any persons who are not health technicians to sell the
medicines and medical products;
6. Have other behaviors which contradict with the laws and regulations
on medicines and medical products.

(New). Prohibitions for Business Operators
The operators of business on medicines and medical products are prohibited to
take the following actions:
1. Operate the business in the non-authorized places;
2. Operate the business without officially authorization;
3. Sell medicines which not included in the relevant list, counterfeit
medicines, substandard medicines, deteriorated medicines and expired
medicines;
4. Advertise over the facts;
5. Transfer or lend the business license or pharmaceutical license;
6. Assign any person who has not officially permission and no knowledge of
pharmaceutical principles to sell the medicines instead of his/her place;
7. Operate the business without medical ethics;
8. Have other behaviors which contradict with the laws and regulations on
medicines and medical products.
